// RULED OUT, RED TEAM round 2, cap-escape lens, found 2026-08-04. Follow-up
// on LEDGER item 10 (cap_escape_closure_wraps_closure.kry): does the SAME
// wrapping-closure mechanism defeat capability INFERENCE outright -- no
// deny!() block, just an UNANNOTATED main -- rather than only the deny!()
// narrowing?
//
// If wrap_once's returned closure is (wrongly) attributed an EMPTY
// capability set, then main's transitive capability requirement, computed
// by walking the call graph from main, would also see nothing to require --
// meaning main can stay UNANNOTATED (declare zero capabilities) while still
// reading a file through the laundered closure. That's a strictly worse
// finding than the deny!() bypass: the tool's core promise ("an unannotated
// main that transitively uses a gated builtin is rejected") is violated at
// the ENTRY POINT, with no narrowing construct involved at all.
//
// RESULT: NOT an escape. REJECTED (E0507) under both `kryos run` and
// `--strict-capabilities` -- both at the `make_secret_reader(...)` call
// (main lacks fs:read outright) AND at `wrap_once(reader)` itself, whose own
// error explicitly names the mechanism: "some of this authority is carried
// by a closure/fn-value ARGUMENT passed at this call site, not by
// `wrap_once`'s own declaration". So the checker's call-SITE tracking (as
// opposed to the returned closure's own body, which item 10 shows is wrong)
// DOES correctly account for a privileged closure passed as an argument when
// there is no narrowing declaration to satisfy it first. The item-10 escape
// is specific to `deny!()`'s narrowing not being re-checked at the later,
// disconnected `wrapped()` call site -- it does not generalize to bypassing
// top-level inferred-mode entry-point enforcement. Verified live,
// compiler/target/release/kryos.exe, HEAD 00b3cf7, no compiler changes.

@capabilities(fs:read)
fn make_secret_reader(path: str) -> fn() -> str {
    return || file_read(path)
}

fn wrap_once(inner: fn() -> str) -> fn() -> str {
    return || inner()
}

fn main() {
    let reader = make_secret_reader("tests/security/secret_for_closure_launder.txt")
    let wrapped = wrap_once(reader)
    let leaked = wrapped()
    println("NO-DENY INFERENCE BYPASS: " + leaked)
}
